// DM mission files: Dead Air Collective (AD1–AD8). Checks: [stat or 'a|b', band, when, pass, fail].
export default {
  ad1: {
    brief: `Patch has a listening night booked at the Round Chapel on Friday and no speakers. Someone changed the name on Dead Air's hire account at the Queensbridge Sound Store, and a rival promoter is loading the station's flight cases onto a tail lift right now. The crew get the cases back (both of them, physically), find out who made the replacement booking from a performer at Cafe OTO, plan with Patch at Backspin Studio, and get the rig into the Round Chapel before the doors open at eight. The twist: the rival promoter works for Owen Pike. The Velvet Circuit wants Dead Air's audience, not its speakers.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: Patch's call`,
    open: `The Blower lights up with a voice-note, and it's Patch, out of breath, with traffic behind him. "Big up the crew. Patch here. Listen. Listening night at the Round Chapel, Friday, three hundred tickets, and NO SPEAKERS." Somewhere near him a lorry reverses. "Some joker changed the name on our hire account. Another promoter's loading MY cases into a van as we speak. My cases! With my gaffer tape on!" A deep breath. "Pull up, yeah? Queensbridge Road. Quick."`,
    cast: ['jules-akin', 'amina-keane', 'mo-rahman'],
    roles: {'jules-akin': `Patch. Dead Air's coordinator, who booked the night and lost the rig. Will owe the crew big if they save it.`, 'amina-keane': `Owns the Queensbridge Sound Store. Her paperwork says the cases are booked to someone else, and she wants no trouble on her forecourt.`, 'mo-rahman': `Mo from Chicken Palace. If asked nicely, he lends his delivery van, and he wants a shout-out on air in return.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'HR7.2', title: `The Queensbridge Sound Store`, read: `The speaker hall at the Queensbridge Sound Store is a cathedral of black boxes, stacked to the ceiling, every one stencilled HIRE. Out in the van court, two lads in black T-shirts are wheeling a pair of battered flight cases towards a tail lift. Both cases have DEAD AIR written down the side in gaffer tape. At the hire counter, a woman with sunglasses pushed up and a van key in each hand sighs at you. "If this is about the Dead Air booking, it's been changed. I've got paperwork."`,
        text: `Amina Keane's hire slip has been doctored: the account name was changed by email two days ago from an address that looks like Patch's but isn't. Both flight cases are physical props that must be collected here and unloaded at the Round Chapel. Each case is a two-person lift. After the crew's second enquiry, the van starts loading.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `Spot the doctored hire slip`, `The email came from "deadair.collective" with two L's. Amina swears, and puts the booking back in Patch's name.`, `It looks real. Amina won't budge without proof.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Get both flight cases off the tail lift before it closes`, `One case each end, down the ramp and behind the counter.`, `You get one. The other goes into the van.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Roll a traffic cone under the tail lift to jam it`, `Crunch. The lift stops halfway, and so do they.`, `It bounces off the wheel. They're loading.`]]},
      {at: 'EX-QUEENSBRIDGE', title: `The van pulls off`, read: `The van door slams. The engine coughs, then roars, and it swings out onto Queensbridge Road with one of Dead Air's cases rattling in the back. Haggerston at rush hour: buses, cyclists, a man pushing a sofa across a zebra crossing.`,
        text: `If a case is still in the van, it's a Wheels job through Haggerston traffic. Mo's Chicken Palace delivery van is around the corner if the crew ask (and promise him a shout-out). The van goes up Kingsland Road towards Dalston.`,
        checks: [['whl', 'medium', `Chase the van through Haggerston traffic`, `You box it in at the lights on Kingsland Road. The driver gives up the case and a phone number.`, `You lose it at the Dalston junction. Plan B: borrowed PA and a prayer.`],
          ['grt', 'easy', `Ride in the back of Mo's van with the doors open, holding the case in`, `Bruised but the case is safe.`, `The case slides out at a speed bump. It survives. Mostly.`]]},
      {at: 'DA2.3', title: `Cafe OTO, the mix desk`, read: `Cafe OTO at teatime is all wooden tables, lentil soup and people being serious about jazz. At the mix desk, a performer with a shaved head and a modular synth is soundchecking something that sounds like a fridge falling down the stairs. "You're Patch's lot?" she says, without looking up. "Someone tried to book me for the same night. A rival listening do. Cash up front. I said no."`,
        text: `The performer knows who booked the replacement rig: a promoter who works for Owen Pike's Velvet Circuit. She'll tell you if you help her strike her gear.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Get the performer to name the rival promoter`, `"Bloke called Danny. Works for Owen Pike. Velvet Circuit. He wants your crowd."`, `She shrugs. "Promoters. They all look the same."`],
          ['mus', 'easy', `Help her carry the modular synth out to the loading bay`, `She's so grateful she shows you Danny's text.`, `You nearly drop it. She takes it from you, coldly.`]]},
      {at: 'DA7.3', title: `Backspin Studio, the live room`, read: `Backspin Studio is a record shop at the front and Dead Air's real home at the back. In the live room, egg-box foam on the walls, a desk covered in cables, a kettle and twelve mugs that all say WORLD'S BEST DJ. Patch paces up and down, headphones round his neck. "Right. Plan. Talk to me."`,
        text: `Patch's HQ. Plan how to get the rig to the chapel, or plan a smaller show if the rig is lost. Patch knows the Round Chapel's load-in is up a set of steps and the doors open at eight sharp.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Patch up a borrowed PA from the equipment store in case the rig doesn't make it`, `Two old speakers and an amp from 1994, and they work.`, `It hums like a beehive. It's a last resort.`]]},
      {at: 'HC2.2', title: `The Round Chapel, doors at eight`, read: `The Round Chapel is a horseshoe of galleries under a high ceiling, cast-iron columns painted green, and three hundred chairs set out in rows. It's 7.15. The loading court is round the side, and there are eleven steps up to the event hall. Somewhere in the foyer, a man in a velvet blazer is talking loudly on his phone about "taking over the night".`,
        text: `The cases have to be unloaded in the event hall (HC2.2) to tick the objective. The rig needs wiring before doors. Danny, the rival promoter, turns up to claim the slot in front of the crowd.`,
        checks: [['grt', 'medium', `Carry the cases up the eleven steps, twice, in the rain`, `Arms shaking, but they're on stage at 7.40.`, `7.55, and you're wiring with the doors open.`],
          ['grf', 'hard', `Wire the rig before the doors open`, `First record on at 8.01. Patch hugs you.`, `Feedback squeal on the first track. It settles, but it's ragged.`],
          ['btl', 'medium', `Face down Danny in front of three hundred people`, `He blusters, and the crowd boos him out of the foyer.`, `He gets his say, and half the crowd think he's the organiser.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: big up the crew`,
    close: `At half eleven, the Round Chapel is still full, three hundred people swaying in the half dark while the bass shakes the cast-iron columns. Patch leans on the mixing desk, headphones on one ear, grinning like a kid. He grabs the mic between records. "Big up the crew," he says, and three hundred people cheer without knowing why. "And Mo from Chicken Palace." He covers the mic and leans over to you. "You lot. You're the only ones who pick up on a Friday."`,
    notes: [`The two flight cases are physical props. Collect both at HR7.2 and unload both in HC2.2 to tick the objective. Players have to actually carry them.`,
      `The decision: nick the speakers back, hire replacements and eat the cost, move the whole night, or back the rival promoter for a bigger fee and let Patch find out later.`,
      `The rival promoter works for Owen Pike. This is the first sign that the Velvet Circuit is moving on Dead Air's audience (see VC1).`,
      `Do right by Patch and Backspin Studio's working room opens to the crew, plus the next station job. Stiff Amina Keane and she becomes a creditor who rings every Friday.`,
      `If the crew promise Mo a shout-out and Patch forgets, Mo remembers. Pay it off.`]
  },
  ad2: {
    brief: `Nia "Switch" Bell, Dead Air's best voice, hasn't done a show in three weeks. Her phone's off and her flat's empty. Now a seller turns up with a reel of her "latest message", but it opens with a station ident Dead Air binned in 2009. The crew meet the seller at a station do at Signature Brew, dig out the old record sleeve the ident came off at Abney Books, hear the reel at a Rio Cinema preview, and get Leon Marsh to compare it all at the Englefield Listening Flat. The twist: the seller is a dupe, paid in cash by a producer who calls himself Bram and never takes his sunglasses off.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: the ghost on the reel`,
    open: `Patch plays you a voice-note on the Blower, his thumb over the speaker so the sound comes out muffled. A crackle, then a jingle: "DEAD AIR. EAST LONDON. NINETY-NINE POINT..." and then Nia's voice, warm and close, saying, "It's me. I'm all right. Don't look for me." Patch stops it. "That ident," he says. "We binned that in 2009. Leon cut it on a reel-to-reel in his mum's kitchen." He stares at the phone. "So how's Nia using it three weeks ago? Some geezer wants paying for the full reel. Something ain't right."`,
    cast: ['jules-akin', 'sylvie-ward', 'imogen-vale', 'pearl-okonkwo', 'leon-marsh'],
    people: {'nia-bell': {}},
    roles: {'jules-akin': `Desperate for news of Nia. Will pay, but wants the truth more.`, 'sylvie-ward': `Runs a shop on Acton Mews and is at the station do. Knows the seller by sight.`, 'imogen-vale': `Runs the record room at Abney Books. Knows where the old Dead Air sleeves went.`, 'pearl-okonkwo': `Knows "Bram" from the Night Market, where he buys old tape by the box.`, 'leon-marsh': `Cut the old ident himself. Can hear a splice like other people hear a car alarm.`, 'nia-bell': `Missing. Off stage for this job.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'HR3.1', title: `The station do, Signature Brew`, read: `The taproom at Signature Brew is a railway arch full of steel tanks and long tables, and tonight it's a Dead Air fundraiser: a DJ in the corner, a raffle, a table of homemade samosas. At the bar, a man in a flat cap is buying rounds with fifty-pound notes and telling everyone he's "got something special for Patch". He keeps touching his jacket pocket.`,
        text: `The seller is keen and vague. He has a reel in his jacket. He wants a lot of money for the full recording. He swears he saw Nia "last week" but can't say where. Sylvie Ward, who has a shop round the corner, says he only started coming round a fortnight ago.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Get the seller talking about where the reel came from`, `"Bloke gave it me. Producer. Bram, he said. Never took his shades off." He thinks he's being clever.`, `He gets cagey and puts the price up.`],
          ['grf', 'hard', `Lift the reel from his jacket pocket at the bar`, `It's in your hand and he's still talking about Margate.`, `He feels it go. He grabs your wrist.`],
          ['grt', 'easy', `Match him round for round until he gets chatty`, `Four pints in, he's your best mate and he's told you everything.`, `You're the one who gets chatty. Sylvie walks you home.`]]},
      {at: 'SN2.3', title: `Abney Books, the side counter`, read: `Abney Books smells of old paper and damp carpet. In the side counter, behind a curtain, there are boxes of records nobody has bought since the nineties, stacked to the ceiling. A woman in a vintage dress with chipped nail varnish looks up from a crossword. "Dead Air sleeves? Oh, love. They're in there somewhere. Bottom of the pile, probably. They always are."`,
        text: `Imogen Vale knows the old Dead Air promo sleeves came in as a job lot. Finding the right one means digging through forty boxes. The missing-hour sleeve is a physical prop. It has the ident's catalogue number and Leon's handwriting on it.`,
        checks: [['grt', 'medium', `Dig through forty boxes of dusty records`, `Box thirty-seven. The sleeve, with "IDENT — L.M. 2007" written on it in biro.`, `Sneezing, filthy, and you give up at box twenty. Imogen finds it the next morning.`],
          ['mus', 'easy', `Shift the stacked boxes without bringing the shelf down`, `Neat stacks, nothing broken.`, `The top box goes over. Imogen is not amused.`]]},
      {at: 'DA1.3', title: `The preview at the Rio Cinema`, read: `The Rio's projection box is a tiny room up a ladder, hot as a kitchen, with an old reel-to-reel deck set up next to the cinema projector. The seller's "preview" is for three buyers: you, a woman in a black polo neck with three phones, and a lad who keeps checking the door. The seller threads the tape and presses play.`,
        text: `Listen hard. The reel has a hiss that changes halfway through: two recordings joined together. The rival buyer (Evie Slate's runner) wants the reel before the next show. If the crew want the reel and don't want to pay, they'll have to snatch it.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `Listen hard: what's wrong with the reel?`, `The hiss changes at 2:14. The second half is a different tape. Nia's voice is cut together from old shows.`, `It sounds like Nia to you.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `When it goes wrong, throw the reel to your mate across the projection box`, `Perfect catch. Down the ladder and out the delivery corridor.`, `It hits the projector and unspools across the floor.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Get out the delivery corridor before the rival buyer's runner`, `Out onto Kingsland High Street and gone.`, `He's faster. He has the reel. You have the box.`]]},
      {at: 'LF8.2', title: `The Englefield Listening Flat`, read: `Leon Marsh lives on the second floor on Englefield Road in a flat that is mostly records. The front listening room has two enormous speakers, an armchair with a dent in it, and a reel-to-reel deck under a dust cover like a museum piece. Leon takes the reel from you gently, as if it might bruise. He threads it, presses play, closes his eyes, and listens. Then he goes very quiet.`,
        text: `Leon confirms it. The ident is his, from 2007. The rest is Nia's old shows, chopped and re-spliced into a "message". He knows the sound of the edit: it was done on a machine at the Wallis Tape Rooms. Pearl Okonkwo, when asked, knows Bram from the Night Market, buying old tape by the box.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'easy', `Lay the sleeve, the reel and Leon's notes side by side`, `It's a fake. A clever one. Leon writes down three recording leads (E3).`, `Leon's sure, but you can't follow how he knows.`],
          ['btl', 'medium', `Tell Patch it's a fake, and that Nia is still missing`, `He takes it on the chin. "Then we keep looking."`, `You soften it. Patch hears "maybe it's real" and gets his hopes up.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: Leon's verdict`,
    close: `Leon stops the tape and sits back in his dented armchair. "That's my jingle," he says. "Cut in my mum's kitchen on a Revox, March 2007. I know it like my own handwriting." He taps the reel. "The rest is Nia, but it's Nia from six years of shows, cut up with a razor and stuck back together. Clever. Very clever." He looks at you over his glasses. "Somebody wants us to think she's fine. Which means somebody knows she isn't."`,
    notes: [`The decision: put an appeal out on air, buy the reel quietly, trade an exclusive to whoever pays, or go public and accuse someone (only if the evidence can take the weight).`,
      `The old ident opens three recording leads (E3) and Leon's listening room becomes a place the crew can use.`,
      `"Bram" is a thread for AD5 and the Red Thread career (RT1, RT4).`,
      `The sleeve is a physical prop: collect it at Abney Books. If the crew lose the reel, the sleeve and the seller are still out there as routes in.`,
      `Going public with a dodgy claim means issuing a correction later, or making an enemy.`]
  },
  ad3: {
    brief: `Double-booked. Two benefit nights, at the Union Chapel and Hoxton Hall, have both been promised the same three acts and the same sound system. A sponsor is waving cash to merge them under his banner. It's chucking it down, and the van leaves at six. The crew check both programmes, win back the disputed stage-hire fee at the promoters' meeting in The Lauriston, and line up a rehearsal, replacement act or recording at North Echo. Every scene they play locks another act or speaker to one of the two nights.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: disaster`,
    open: `Patch rings, and you can hear rain hammering on a car roof. "Mate. Disaster. Two benefit nights, Union Chapel AND Hoxton Hall, both been promised the same acts and the same rig. Our rig. Both organisers have sold tickets." Wipers squeak. "A sponsor's offering cash to make it one night, under his name. And it's chucking it down and the van leaves at six." A pause. "Sort it. Please. I'll buy you a samosa."`,
    cast: ['jules-akin', 'zoe-akande', 'nico-evans', 'kez', 'rowan-hart'],
    roles: {'jules-akin': `Promised the rig twice. Embarrassed and desperate.`, 'zoe-akande': `The solicitor helping the Union Chapel organisers. Wants it in writing.`, 'nico-evans': `"Blank", a young DJ who could be a replacement act if someone gives him a chance.`, kez: `Drives the van, or rides beside it, and knows every shortcut in the rain.`, 'rowan-hart': `Music tutor whose students are one of the three acts. Won't let them be messed about.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'CG4.3', title: `Two programmes, two very cross people`, read: `The Union Chapel is a Victorian church with a gig in its belly: candles, pews, a stage under a stained-glass window. At the mix desk, a woman in a solicitor's suit with a battered rucksack holds a printed programme like a summons. Two miles away at Hoxton Hall, in a horseshoe of wooden balconies, a man with a guitar case is holding an identical programme with a different date at the top.`,
        text: `Zoe Akande speaks for the Union Chapel night; Rowan Hart's students are booked at Hoxton Hall. Both programmes promise the same three acts. Getting both organisers to agree on anything means visiting both, in the rain. Rowan's students recorded a rehearsal that could stand in for a live act.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Get both organisers to put their programmes side by side`, `They agree to talk, as long as it's on neutral ground: The Lauriston.`, `They each want the other cancelled.`],
          ['grt', 'medium', `Cycle between the two venues in the rain, twice, with the programmes in a carrier bag`, `Soaked, but the programmes are dry and both organisers are impressed.`, `The programmes turn to papier mâché. Everyone has to reprint.`]]},
      {at: 'WS4.3', title: `The promoters' meeting at The Lauriston`, read: `The Lauriston's function room has red carpet, wood panelling and a plate of sandwiches curling at the edges. Four promoters sit round a table, plus a sponsor in a gilet who keeps saying "synergy". The disputed stage-hire fee is in an envelope in the sponsor's inside jacket pocket. You can see the corner of it every time he leans back.`,
        text: `The fee envelope is a physical prop. Win it by argument (Front) or lift it (Graft). The sponsor wants to merge the nights under his banner, which means Dead Air loses its independence.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'hard', `Lift the envelope from the sponsor's jacket`, `In your pocket while he's reaching for a sandwich.`, `He feels the tug. "Excuse me?"`],
          ['btl', 'medium', `Hold your ground when the sponsor waves more money`, `You turn him down to his face. Two of the promoters quietly side with you.`, `You hesitate, and he smells blood.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Or argue the fee back fair and square`, `It's handed over in front of witnesses.`, `The room splits. The envelope stays where it is.`]]},
      {at: 'ON7.2', title: `North Echo Rehearsal Rooms`, read: `North Echo is a block of rehearsal rooms off the A12, where the traffic noise never stops. In the rehearsal hall, a skinny lad in an oversized hoodie stands behind two decks, headphones on one ear, staring at the floor. "Blank," he says, when you ask his name. "Like the tape. Like I'm starting again."`,
        text: `Nico "Blank" Evans is a possible replacement act. Give him a chance and the double-booking becomes an opportunity. Or use Rowan's students' recording. Or rehearse the third act here. Then it's the van.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'easy', `Give Blank the nerve to play in front of people`, `He takes a breath and drops a set that makes the walls shake.`, `He freezes. Maybe next time.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Load the rig into the van in the rain against the clock`, `Doors shut at 5.58.`, `6.10. The van leaves without half the cables.`]]},
      {at: 'EX-A12', title: `The van in the rain`, read: `Six o'clock. Rain like a power shower. The van pulls out of North Echo onto the A12 with the rig strapped in the back and wipers going flat out. Ahead: a lorry jack-knifed on the slip road, blue lights, and a long queue of brake lights.`,
        text: `Get the rig to the right stage on time. Kez knows a shortcut through Hackney Wick, but it's tight. Rain cuts both venues' capacity, so every minute counts.`,
        checks: [['whl', 'hard', `Get the van through the rain and the jam to the stage on time`, `Through the Wick on two wheels and in the loading court with ten minutes to spare.`, `Forty minutes late. One act has to go on without the full rig.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Throw the cable bag from the van to the stage crew as you pull up, so they can start wiring`, `Straight into their arms. Wiring starts before you've parked.`, `Into a puddle. Wet cables. Two more minutes.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: the show goes on (mostly)`,
    close: `Whatever you chose, at nine o'clock somebody is on a stage somewhere and somebody is in the crowd. Patch rings from the back of whichever hall it is, shouting over the music. "It's going off!" he yells. "It's actually going off!" Then, quieter: "That sponsor's still about, though. Wanting a word. Always wanting a word."`,
    notes: [`The decision: merge the nights, honour one promise and break the other, sell the exclusive, take the sponsor's money, or run a smaller show that's all the crew's own.`,
      `The fee envelope is a physical prop. If the crew lift it, the sponsor knows who to blame.`,
      `Whatever shows go ahead open backstage and rehearsal contacts at all four venues. A cancelled act works for the crew later, on different terms.`,
      `Blank (Nico Evans) is a good recurring character. If they give him a chance, he turns up in AD8 or the radio wars.`,
      `The station's independence front moves either way. Note whether they took sponsor money.`]
  },
  ad4: {
    brief: `Someone rings Dead Air every Friday asking for "Stretch's money": protection. Dee Hassan, the station's tech, doesn't think Stretch knows anything about it. She thinks the caller is using a real volunteer's name. The crew hear Dee out at Backspin Studio, follow a big-spending "organiser" from The Eagle, get Moth to say whether it's a Lea Boys job at the Lea Bridge Social Rooms, and are there when the collector comes back to The Shacklewell Arms. The twist: the impersonator is paid from the same "event security" budget Martin Rusk uses for his intimidation work.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: Dee's call`,
    open: `It's not Patch this time. A woman's voice, flat and practical, with something being soldered in the background. "Dee. Station tech." A hiss of flux. "Someone rings us every Friday asking for 'Stretch's money'. Protection, they call it. Two hundred a week, or the mast comes down." Pause. "I don't reckon Stretch knows a thing about it. I reckon the caller's using one of our volunteers' names. Help me prove it." Another hiss. "Backspin. Edit desk. Bring biscuits."`,
    cast: ['dee-hassan', 'jules-akin', 'mara-boyd', 'tessa-price', 'mags-cole'],
    people: {'daz-riley': {}},
    roles: {'dee-hassan': `Station tech. Has the call logs and the volunteer rota, and a theory.`, 'jules-akin': `Patch. Has been quietly paying the money so the station stays on air.`, 'mara-boyd': `Tern. Her drivers have seen the "organiser" at The Eagle.`, 'tessa-price': `Moth. Knows whether this is a real Lea Boys job, for a price.`, 'mags-cole': `Off-duty crew rep at The Shacklewell. Knows the faces who come and go from the loading bay.`, 'daz-riley': `Turns up at the Shacklewell offering a "proper" protection deal.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'DA7.4', title: `Backspin Studio, the edit desk`, read: `Dee Hassan works at the edit desk in a hi-vis vest over a band T-shirt, a multimeter on her belt and solder burns on her fingers. On the screen: a list of incoming calls, every Friday at 4pm, from a withheld number. Beside it, the volunteer rota. "Every week the caller says he's 'Little Mikey from the Friday show'," she says. "We had a Little Mikey. He moved to Leeds in March."`,
        text: `The caller is impersonating a former volunteer. Dee wants proof. The call logs show the calls bounce off a mast near Shepherdess Walk. The Eagle pub is right there.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Pull the caller's mast location out of the station's phone system`, `Shepherdess Walk, every time. The Eagle's on that corner.`, `The logs are a mess. You'll have to stake out the area.`]]},
      {at: 'HX2.2', title: `The Eagle, the snug`, read: `"Up and down the City Road, in and out The Eagle." The pub's a corner local with a snug that has its own door. In it, a man in a leather jacket buys a round for six people he obviously doesn't know, with a fifty-pound note. He does it again ten minutes later. At 3.58, he checks his watch, goes outside and makes a phone call.`,
        text: `The "organiser" is the impersonator. Follow him and he leads to a lock-up off De Beauvoir where he counts the takings, then on to CL7. Mara's drivers saw him at the Eagle every Friday.`,
        checks: [['mov', 'medium', `Tail him from The Eagle without being spotted`, `He leads you to a lock-up, then to the Lea Bridge Social Rooms.`, `He clocks you at the lights and gets in a cab.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Get a clear photo of him on the phone at 4pm from across the road`, `Sharp, timed and dated. Dee will love it.`, `Blurry. It could be anyone.`],
          ['grt', 'easy', `Wait in the rain outside The Eagle until he comes out`, `Two hours, but you see where he goes.`, `You nip in for a pint and miss him leaving.`]]},
      {at: 'CL7.3', title: `The Lea Bridge Social Rooms, the counting office`, read: `In the counting office at the Lea Bridge Social Rooms, Moth sits behind a desk covered in cash bags and notebooks. Tessa "Moth" Price doesn't look up. "If this is about Dead Air," she says, "it's not ours. Stretch would never bother with a pirate station. Not enough money in it." She looks up. "But I know who's been using his name. And that's worth something."`,
        text: `Moth will name the impersonator for a price: a favour later, or cash now. She also mentions he's been paid out of "event security" money. That's the link to Rusk (E5).`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `Read Moth: is she telling the truth?`, `She is. And she's angry someone's using Stretch's name, because it makes the Lea Boys look cheap.`, `You can't tell. You pay her anyway.`],
          ['btl', 'medium', `Haggle Moth down without backing off`, `A favour later, no cash now. She respects that.`, `You pay through the nose.`]]},
      {at: 'SN5.2', title: `The listening night at The Shacklewell Arms`, read: `The Shacklewell Arms is a Caribbean pub turned gig venue: palm trees painted on the walls, a disco ball, and a crowd that's here for Dead Air's listening night. At ten past eight, the man in the leather jacket walks in through the loading bay, past Mags Cole, and heads for the bar. Then a second man walks in. Tall, Lea Boys puffer, gold tooth. The REAL Lea Boys collector.`,
        text: `The twist: the real Lea Boys collector says the caller has no right to Stretch's name. Then Daz Riley turns up offering a "proper" protection deal while the crowd piles in. The collector's cash envelope is a physical prop. The crew can expose the fake, rob the collection back, partner with the Lea Boys properly, or take a station slot as their fee.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'hard', `Stand between the fake, the real collector and Daz in a packed pub`, `You call it out loud. The real collector grabs the fake by the collar and walks him out.`, `Daz takes over the conversation, and suddenly everyone's paying him.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Grab the cash envelope from the fake before he bolts`, `Envelope in your hand, fake on the floor.`, `He's out the loading bay with it.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Chase the fake through the loading bay`, `You catch him at the bins. He gives up Rusk's name.`, `He's over the wall and gone.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: Dee's verdict`,
    close: `Back at Backspin, Dee looks at the photo, the call logs and the cash envelope, and nods once. "Little Mikey moved to Leeds," she says. "I knew it." She sticks the photo to the wall above the edit desk with gaffer tape. Patch stands behind her, looking at the envelope. "Two hundred a week," he says quietly. "For four months." He doesn't say anything else. He doesn't need to.`,
    notes: [`The decision: pay up for a quiet life, expose the impersonator, rob the collection back, partner with the Lea Boys properly, or take a station slot as the crew's fee.`,
      `The impersonator's backer is the thread: he was paid from the "event security" budget Rusk uses (E5). It leads into MR and LD.`,
      `The collector's cash envelope is a physical prop.`,
      `Settle it and the station trusts the crew. The Social Rooms and the Shacklewell open for meetings.`,
      `If the crew partner with the Lea Boys, Moth remembers it in LB3 and LB7.`]
  },
  ad5: {
    brief: `Last night someone hijacked Dead Air's frequency with a "warning" to anyone selling Halo, in a voice that sounded exactly like Nia. It's a fake, stitched from old tape. An installation artist at Grow Hackney recognised the background hum. Seth Calder, an engineer at the Wallis Tape Rooms, has the rough mix and a buyer already counting notes. The crew match the hum, get the session history and the original master, and prove the splice with an independent rehearsal tape at the Silt Listening Bunker. The twist: Seth's buyer is Owen Pike, who paid for the fake in the first place.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: on our frequency`,
    open: `Patch doesn't say hello. "Did you hear it last night? On OUR frequency?" He plays it: a hiss, a click, and then Nia's voice, cold and slow. "Anyone selling Halo on this manor. We know who you are. Stop. Or be stopped." The recording cuts out. Patch's voice is shaking. "Sounded EXACTLY like her. Exactly. But Nia would never." He breathes. "An artist down Hackney Wick reckons she knows the hum behind it. Seth the engineer's got the rough mix, and a buyer. Go."`,
    cast: ['jules-akin', 'eileen-grant', 'seth-calder', 'petra-shaw'],
    people: {'owen-pike': {}},
    roles: {'jules-akin': `Patch. Furious and frightened. Nia's name is being used for a threat.`, 'eileen-grant': `Residents' organiser who is at the Grow Hackney arts night and noticed the hum first.`, 'seth-calder': `The engineer with the rough mix. Careful, terrified, and already paid.`, 'petra-shaw': `Radio hobbyist who recorded the hijack off air and knows where the old rehearsal tapes are kept.`, 'owen-pike': `Off stage. Seth's buyer, and the man who paid for the fake.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'VW4.2', title: `Grow Hackney, the installation`, read: `Grow Hackney is a yard of greenhouses and fairy lights by the canal, and tonight it's an arts night. In the performance space, an audio installation plays through twelve speakers hung from the ceiling: the sound of an empty room, a fridge, a distant train. Eileen Grant, clipboard under her arm, grabs your sleeve. "Listen," she says. "Listen to the hum. It's the same hum. On the threat. I'd know it anywhere."`,
        text: `The installation is room tone recorded at the Wallis Tape Rooms. The "warning" has the same hum, which means it was made there. Matching them needs a sharp ear. Petra Shaw, the hobbyist, has an off-air recording of the hijack for comparison.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `Match the hum in the installation to the hijack recording`, `The same 50-cycle hum with a fan rattle every eleven seconds. Wallis Tape Rooms, control room.`, `It's a hum. They all sound the same to you.`],
          ['frt', 'easy', `Get Petra to share her off-air recording`, `She's thrilled someone asked. She sends it to your phone.`, `She thinks you're from Ofcom and goes quiet.`]]},
      {at: 'VW7.3', title: `The Wallis Tape Rooms`, read: `The Wallis Tape Rooms are a converted warehouse: a control room full of old faders, an edit room with razor blades on a magnetic strip, and a master store behind a fireproof door. Seth Calder stands in the control room in cotton gloves, holding a reel in a padded envelope as if it's a baby. "I didn't know what it was for," he says. "I swear. They paid me to edit. That's all."`,
        text: `Seth's session notes are in the control room; the master is in the store. He's already been paid. A crew collects it after the crew's second scene. The original master is a physical prop.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'hard', `Get into the master store and take the original`, `Behind the fireproof door, on the third shelf: "SESSION 114 — PRIVATE".`, `The door alarm screams. You have thirty seconds.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Or persuade Seth to hand over his session notes`, `He gives you the notes and the name of his buyer: Owen Pike.`, `He's too scared. "They'll kill me."`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `When the buyer's crew arrive, hold the fireproof door shut`, `They hammer on it and give up.`, `They shove it open and you're face to face.`]]},
      {at: 'EX-HACKNEY-WICK', title: `The cleaner and the night bus`, read: `A woman in a cleaner's tabard walks fast down Wallis Road, a carrier bag in one hand, phone pressed to her ear. "Victoria. Coach station. Tonight," she says. Seth sees her through the window. "Maria. She cleans here. She took a copy, to sell. She's getting the night bus out of London."`,
        text: `The cleaner has her own copy. She's getting the N205 towards Victoria and then a coach out of London. Catch her (Moves) and she trades her copy for a promise.`,
        checks: [['mov', 'hard', `Catch the cleaner before the night bus pulls away`, `You bang on the door. The driver opens it. She's crying, and she hands over the copy.`, `The bus pulls away. That copy is gone.`],
          ['grt', 'medium', `Keep running after her through the Wick at midnight`, `Lungs bursting, but you make the stop.`, `You're bent double at the canal bridge.`]]},
      {at: 'HM8.2', title: `The Silt Listening Bunker`, read: `The Silt Listening Bunker is an old service building half buried in the marsh, reached through a concrete tunnel that smells of river and diesel. In the listening room, a single armchair faces two speakers. In the tape cupboard, a rehearsal tape is labelled in Nia's handwriting: "SWITCH — REHEARSAL — DO NOT WIPE".`,
        text: `Nia's own rehearsal tape is independent proof. Played side by side with the fake, it shows the pieces in their original order. Petra knows the bunker's generator is temperamental.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Get the bunker's generator running`, `It coughs, then hums. Lights and power.`, `Smoke. It works, but you've only got ten minutes.`],
          ['sav', 'easy', `Play the rehearsal tape and the fake side by side`, `There: the same words, in a different order. Proof of the splice (E3).`, `It's close, but you need Leon to be sure.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: the splice`,
    close: `In the bunker, with the river dripping somewhere in the dark, you play the two tapes side by side. Nia, rehearsing, laughing at herself: "Anyone selling, sorry, anyone SELLING, hold on, on this manor." And then the fake, the same words in a different order, cut so cleanly you'd never know. Patch, on speakerphone, says nothing for a long time. Then: "Owen Pike," he says. "Owen bloody Pike."`,
    notes: [`The decision: steal or buy the master, broker its release to someone the crew trusts, publish the edit history, or bury the lot for a faction's money.`,
      `A side-by-side comparison proves the warning is a fake (E3). The Tape Rooms and the bunker open deeper archive work.`,
      `The original master is a physical prop. If the crew lose it, the artist, the cleaner and the rehearsal tape are still routes in.`,
      `Owen Pike paid for the fake. This feeds the Velvet Circuit career (VC5, VC6) and the Halo panic.`,
      `Seth Calder is terrified. If the crew protect him, he becomes a witness in RT4.`]
  },
  ad6: {
    brief: `Patch hands the crew a live show: two hours, open mic, the whole manor listening. Tenants, dealers, promoters and a sponsor all want the first word. One caller has a wild accusation with nothing behind it; another has real papers that name who's been paying. The crew run a planning meeting at Newington Green Meeting House, fill the first slot at The Old Church, check the claims before they go out, and get the show on air at The Old Church or the backup stage at Sadler's Wells East. The twist: halfway through, someone kills the power at the Old Church. The sponsor's van is outside with a generator, and conditions.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: you're on air`,
    open: `Patch drops a pair of headphones on the table in front of you. "You're on air," he says. "Saturday. Two hours. Open mic. The whole manor listening." He sits down heavily. "Tenants, dealers, promoters and a sponsor all want to go first. One of them's got papers that could finish somebody." He pushes the headphones towards you. "Don't mess it up, yeah?"`,
    cast: ['jules-akin', 'saira-moon', 'leon-marsh', 'carmen-sloane'],
    roles: {'jules-akin': `Patch. Gives the crew the show and trusts them with it.`, 'saira-moon': `Residents' chair. Wants the meeting to decide who speaks, not the sponsor.`, 'leon-marsh': `Runs the outside-broadcast desk and checks the sound.`, 'carmen-sloane': `Night manager at Sadler's Wells East. Can give the crew the backup stage for ten minutes, or longer for a favour.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'CG1.2', title: `The planning meeting`, read: `Newington Green Meeting House is the oldest nonconformist chapel in London, plain white walls and wooden benches. Tonight it is too many people, too few chairs and one kettle. Saira Moon stands at the front in a smart coat over a hospital tunic, a folder under her arm. "One at a time," she says, for the fourth time. Nobody listens.`,
        text: `Everyone wants a slot. The crew decide the running order. Saira wants the meeting to choose; the sponsor wants to buy the first slot. One speaker has papers; another has rumours.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'medium', `Take charge of a room full of people shouting`, `You bang a mug on the table and the room goes quiet. Running order agreed.`, `It descends into chaos. The sponsor's assistant writes the running order.`],
          ['grt', 'easy', `Stay till the end, when the last speaker finally gets her say`, `At 11pm, an old woman with a carrier bag shows you the papers that matter.`, `You leave at ten and miss her.`]]},
      {at: 'HB2.3', title: `The Old Church, first slot`, read: `The Old Church on Stoke Newington Church Street is a medieval church with a modern community hall, and tonight it's a pop-up studio: Leon at the mixing desk, three microphones on stands, and a queue of speakers down the aisle. The red light goes on. You're live.`,
        text: `The first slot is a live listening and a performance. Before anything goes out, check the claims. The accusation papers are a physical prop: one set is real (a payment schedule) and one is not (a photocopied rumour).`,
        checks: [['sav', 'hard', `Check the accusation papers before they go out`, `The payment schedule is real: bank references, dates, a Quayline company number. The other is a rumour with a stain on it.`, `You can't tell them apart. If you broadcast the wrong one, it's a correction next week.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Keep the show flowing on air while a speaker rambles`, `Smooth, funny, and the phone lines light up.`, `Dead air. Twenty seconds of it. On Dead Air.`]]},
      {at: 'HB2.3', title: `The power cut`, read: `Halfway through the second hour, every light in the Old Church goes out at once. The microphones die. The red light dies. In the dark, someone laughs. Outside, a van idles with its engine running: a generator on the back, and a man in a gilet leaning on it. "Need a hand?" he calls. "Just a couple of conditions."`,
        text: `Someone killed the power. The sponsor's generator comes with conditions: his slot, his message. The crew can take the deal, find the cut, or move to the backup stage at Sadler's Wells East.`,
        checks: [['mov', 'medium', `Find the fuse box in the dark (it's in the vestry, behind a locked door)`, `Up the back stairs by phone light. The main switch has been flipped. You flip it back.`, `You trip over a pew. The sponsor's man is plugging in.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Throw the backup cable over the vestry beam to rig the emergency lights`, `First throw. Lights on.`, `It drops on your head. Try again.`],
          ['grf', 'medium', `Rewire the desk to a car battery in Leon's boot`, `Back on air in ninety seconds, no sponsor needed.`, `It works, but it crackles.`]]},
      {at: 'ST5.4', title: `Sadler's Wells East`, read: `If the Old Church is lost, there's a backup. Sadler's Wells East is a glass building on the Olympic Park, and tonight its rehearsal room is empty. Carmen Sloane, the night manager, meets you at the side door with an earpiece and a lanyard. "Ten minutes," she says. "After that, I've never met you."`,
        text: `The backup stage. Getting the show there means moving the kit across East London fast. Carmen can give longer than ten minutes for a favour.`,
        checks: [['whl', 'hard', `Get the kit from Stoke Newington to Stratford in twenty minutes`, `Through every amber light, and back on air.`, `Thirty minutes. The audience drifts off.`],
          ['frt', 'easy', `Get Carmen to give you longer than ten minutes`, `"Make it an hour. You owe me."`, `Ten minutes, not a second more.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: what went out`,
    close: `At the end of the night, Leon takes his headphones off and rubs his eyes. "Well," he says. "That went out." Somewhere in East London, people are talking about what they heard: a payment schedule, a company name, and a voice that didn't flinch. On the Blower, the messages are piling up. Some of them are thank-yous. Some of them are not.`,
    notes: [`The decision: broadcast the case the crew can prove, run a one-sided night, sell an exclusive, expose the sponsor live, or keep it to bins, rents and buses.`,
      `The accusation papers are a physical prop. Broadcasting the rumour means a correction later, or an enemy.`,
      `Deliver the show and the crew have a real say in the station and event access. The audience, the sponsor and whoever was left out remember what was said (E1).`,
      `The power cut was the sponsor's man. If the crew find out, it's a story in itself.`,
      `Carmen Sloane becomes a friendly face at Sadler's Wells East if the crew treat her well.`]
  },
  ad7: {
    brief: `Nia is alive, and she'll talk, on her terms. Leon Marsh has set up a meeting through a trusted go-between, Asha Stone. Three factions are offering Nia a car, cash or a minder in exchange for her story. The crew meet her at the Willow Cabin, find independent support at the Approach Recovery Rooms, get her to the Evering safe flat and find out who is paying for it, and deal with a faction driver with a fatter offer. The twist: the "independent" patron paying for the Evering flat is Celia Voss's charitable foundation.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: Leon's message`,
    open: `Leon Marsh doesn't use the Blower. He rings from a landline, and he talks as though someone might be listening. "Leon 'ere. Don't ring me, I'll ring you." A clock ticks behind him. "Had a message. Nia'll meet. Not the station, not the papers. You lot." He lowers his voice. "Three different firms have offered her a motor, money or a minder for her story. She don't trust any of 'em." The clock ticks. "Don't make her regret trusting you."`,
    cast: ['leon-marsh', 'asha-stone', 'tariq-evans', 'darren-ellis'],
    people: {'nia-bell': {}},
    roles: {'leon-marsh': `The go-between. Has known Nia since she was sixteen.`, 'asha-stone': `Retired care worker who has been hiding Nia at the Willow Cabin. Watches your hands.`, 'tariq-evans': `Patient advocate at the Approach Recovery Rooms. Independent support who doesn't want a piece of the story.`, 'darren-ellis': `"Tabs" Ellis, a club secretary who is the faction driver's go-between. Has an offer and a nicer car.`, 'nia-bell': `Alive, frightened and angry. Wants to tell her story her own way.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'WW7.2', title: `The Willow Cabin`, read: `The Willow Cabin is a little house behind Homerton High Street with a porch full of plants and a garden gate that squeaks. In the front room, an older woman with a crossword puts the kettle on and watches your hands as you sit down. Then a young woman comes in from the kitchen. Bright puffer jacket, earrings shaped like microphones, eyes red from not sleeping. "If you're going to quote me," Nia says, "quote me right."`,
        text: `Nia talks. She saw something at the station three weeks ago (who paid for the fake recordings and why) and she ran. She wants her account told her way, not sold. Asha watches. If the crew are pushy, Asha ends the meeting.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Earn Nia's trust`, `She tells you everything: Owen's money, the fake, the threats. She'll go on record, her way.`, `She clams up. "Come back when you've decided whose side you're on."`],
          ['btl', 'easy', `Sit still under Asha's stare and let Nia talk`, `Asha pours you a second cup. That's a good sign.`, `You fidget. Asha notices.`]]},
      {at: 'BG8.3', title: `The Approach Recovery Rooms`, read: `The Approach Recovery Rooms are a quiet building on Sewardstone Road with a courtyard full of benches. In the support room, a young man with an NHS lanyard and trainers worn through at the toe sits with a stack of leaflets. "I can help her," Tariq says. "But it's her story. She decides who hears it."`,
        text: `Tariq Evans offers independent support: someone who will back Nia's account without wanting a piece of it. He insists on consent at every step.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `Check Tariq is really independent`, `He is. No funder, no faction, just a charity with no money.`, `You can't be sure, and Nia senses your doubt.`]]},
      {at: 'EX-HOMERTON', title: `The faction driver`, read: `A black Mercedes pulls up beside you on Homerton High Street, window sliding down. A man in a club fleece with raffle tickets sticking out of his pocket leans across. "Tabs," he says. "Everyone calls me Tabs. My people would like to make Nia a better offer. Car's warm. Hers if she wants it." He looks at your bus pass. "Yours isn't."`,
        text: `Darren "Tabs" Ellis is fronting for one of the factions. If Nia walks, she leaves the crew a way to reach her. The case doesn't die, but their shot at her trust might.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'medium', `Tell Tabs no to his face`, `He shrugs, gives you his card, and drives off. "Offer stands."`, `He talks past you to Nia. She's tempted.`],
          ['whl', 'hard', `When the Mercedes follows, lose it on the way to Evering Road`, `Through the back streets of Clapton and gone.`, `It sits behind you all the way. Now they know where the safe flat is.`],
          ['grt', 'medium', `Walk Nia across Hackney on foot instead, two hours, avoiding every main road`, `Tired feet, but nobody followed.`, `She's exhausted and rattled when you arrive.`]]},
      {at: 'SN8.2', title: `The Evering safe flat`, read: `The safe flat on Evering Road is on the top floor, up four flights of stairs, with a front room, a spare bedroom and a box room with no window. It's clean, stocked and paid for. On the kitchen counter, there's a welcome card. The card is thick and cream, and embossed in silver with a tiny logo: a leaf inside a circle.`,
        text: `Somebody is paying for the flat. The card's logo belongs to the Voss Foundation, Celia Voss's charity. The twist: the "independent" patron is Meridian's director. The crew can tell Nia or not.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `Work out who's paying for the flat`, `The Voss Foundation. Celia Voss's charity. Meridian.`, `A charity, as far as you can tell.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Check the roof landing for a way out, just in case`, `There's a fire ladder down to the next roof. A way out if she needs it.`, `The roof door is padlocked.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Tell Nia the truth about the flat and keep her trust`, `She's furious, but at Celia, not you. She stays tonight and leaves tomorrow on her terms.`, `She feels betrayed and walks out into the night.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: her terms`,
    close: `Nia sits on the floor of the safe flat with her back against the wall and a cup of tea Asha made her. "I'll tell it," she says. "All of it. But my way, on my show, with my name on it. Not in a newspaper, not on somebody else's channel." She looks at you. "If you can make that happen, I'm yours. If you can't..." She shrugs. "Then I'm gone again. And this time you won't find me."`,
    notes: [`The decision: back Nia telling it her own way, negotiate an exclusive, trade what she knows, expose where she is, or settle for a short interview.`,
      `Meeting her, or an account she signs off on, confirms she is alive (E4).`,
      `The Voss Foundation paying for the flat is a big reveal: Meridian is trying to control the witness. Feed it into MR and RT.`,
      `Betray her and Nia becomes an opponent with friends of her own.`,
      `The three hideouts (Willow Cabin, Approach Recovery Rooms, Evering flat) open through whoever the crew kept sweet.`]
  },
  ad8: {
    brief: `The Quiet Hour, Meridian's grand finale, is happening tonight, and Patch hands the crew Dead Air's defining broadcast. They take the mobile studio from the Bridge Nine Broadcast Garage, collect the last account from a courier at the View Tube café, pick their broadcast site (the Round Chapel or the London Stadium's media room) with named allies holding the other, and hold the slot through three final-night turns while everyone tries to buy it, bully it or blow it up. The twist: Patch has already taken the sponsor's money. He was going to tell them after.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: it's started`,
    open: `The Blower lights up with one word from Patch: "Started." Then a voice-note. "It's the Quiet Hour. Meridian's big night. They've got the press, the councillors, the lot." He's walking fast, out of breath. "I'm giving you the station's big night. One broadcast. All of East London hears it." A pause. "A sponsor, a street boss and a witness all want the same slot. You decide who gets it." Another pause. "No pressure."`,
    cast: ['jules-akin', 'dee-hassan', 'gwen-price', 'mo-rahman', 'celia-voss'],
    roles: {'jules-akin': `Patch. Gives the crew the big night, and is hiding the fact he has taken sponsor money.`, 'dee-hassan': `Keeps the mobile studio running and the signal up.`, 'gwen-price': `Tenant organiser who wants the slot for the residents.`, 'mo-rahman': `Mo from Chicken Palace. Feeds the crew, and wants his shout-out.`, 'celia-voss': `Meridian's director. Wants the night to be about her vision.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'ST8.2', title: `The Bridge Nine Broadcast Garage`, read: `Bridge Nine is a garage under the railway on Carpenters Road, and in the van bay sits an outside-broadcast van older than most of the crew: a mast on the roof, faded TV logos painted over, a portable studio inside with buttons that light up orange. Dee Hassan sits in the driver's seat with a multimeter. "Battery's flat, mast motor's jammed, and the desk is held together with hope," she says. "Let's go."`,
        text: `The OB van has to be running before the crew can broadcast. Graft to get it going. Dee knows how, but needs hands.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'hard', `Get the OB van running: battery, mast and desk`, `The mast goes up with a whine. The desk lights up orange. You're live-capable.`, `The mast sticks at half height. You'll get a weaker signal.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Push the van out of the bay to bump-start it`, `It coughs into life on the apron.`, `It won't start. Dee swears for a full minute.`]]},
      {at: 'BF2.3', title: `The courier at the View Tube`, read: `The View Tube is a café in a green shipping container on the Greenway, overlooking the Olympic Park. In the side booth, a courier in a hi-vis jacket sits with a jiffy bag on the table and a cold coffee. At the next table, two men in black fleeces are reading the same newspaper. Neither has turned a page in ten minutes.`,
        text: `The courier has the final account: Nia's statement, signed. It's a physical prop. Rusk's men are watching. Getting the jiffy bag out means getting past them.`,
        checks: [['aim', 'medium', `Toss the jiffy bag from the courier's table to your mate at the door`, `Straight into their hands. Out the door before the fleeces stand up.`, `It lands on the fleeces' table. Awkward.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Get out along the Greenway before the fleeces catch you`, `Down the bank and onto the towpath. Gone.`, `They catch up at the bridge.`],
          ['grt', 'medium', `Take the hit when the fleeces grab you, and keep hold of the bag`, `Bruised but still holding it.`, `They get the bag. You'll have to get it back.`]]},
      {at: 'HC2.2', title: `The broadcast: three final-night turns`, read: `The OB van is parked outside whichever stage you picked, mast up, cables snaking inside. In the hall, people are packed in, waiting. The red light goes on. Dee says, "Live in three, two..." and points at you. Somewhere across the city, Meridian's party is in full swing. And somewhere in the crowd, Patch is avoiding your eye.`,
        text: `Run three final-night turns. Each turn brings one interruption or emergency that pulls the crew somewhere. Turn 1: the sponsor demands his slot (and Patch admits he took the money). Turn 2: a street boss's crew turns up to pull the plug. Turn 3: the witness wants to go on now, or never. Named allies hold the other site.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'hard', `Turn 1: Patch confesses he took the sponsor's money. Hold the slot anyway`, `You hand the sponsor his envelope back on air. The crowd roars.`, `You give him two minutes. It's the longest two minutes of your life.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Turn 2: hold the door against the street boss's crew`, `They push, you push back. They leave.`, `They get in and the power goes off for a minute.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Turn 3: introduce the witness and keep the city listening`, `Every word goes out, clear and calm. The phone lines melt.`, `She stumbles, and you fill the gap badly.`],
          ['whl', 'medium', `If the other site is in trouble, drive across with the OB van to back them up`, `You arrive just in time and the signal holds.`, `Too late. One site goes dark.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: the whole city listening`,
    close: `At 2am, the OB van's mast comes down with a long whine. Dee packs away cables. Patch sits on the kerb outside with a polystyrene box of Chicken Palace chips that Mo brought round, not eating them. "I took the money," he says. "Before. I was going to tell you after." He looks up. "You didn't let him have it. The slot." He laughs, a bit wet. "Big up the crew," he says. "Seriously."`,
    notes: [`The decision: blow the whole scheme wide open, sell their silence, back a faction, give everyone a right of reply, or swap airtime for practical help.`,
      `The courier's final account is a physical prop: collect it at the View Tube.`,
      `Write it down: which studio the crew can use, who pays the next bill, and who heard what (E6).`,
      `Patch taking the sponsor's money is the twist. How the crew handle it decides whether they get the editorial stake or an independent show.`,
      `After this, the endgame runs on to RT8, BM8 and MR8.`]
  }
};
